  "body": "As far as I can tell, the idea is that, in the window case of `DrawingStyle`, you take the `Window` the constructor came from, then you get the active document in the browsing context, and use its `Document` as the \"styles scope object\".  Later this \"styles scope object\" is used for font styles and in particular for resolving font family names to things pulled in via CSS `@font-face`.\n\nThe setup for `CanvasRenderingContext2D` is similar, but using its prototype object instead of the constructor.\n\nThe problem, of course, is that there's a cross-origin issue here.  If I grab a `DrawingStyle` constructor from a same-origin page, then navigate that browsing context to some cross-origin URI, then call the constructor, suddenly I've got a `DrawingStyle` object that's reading font information from the cross-origin document.  This is clearly not ok.\n\nI don't understand why we're looking at the active document at all.  Once we've found our way to a `Window`, why not use its \"newest document\" for the `Document` we want?\n",
